<BaseLayout title="The Birth of the United States" description="Explore how thirteen British colonies declared independence and built a new republic, while learning practical American English through history.">
  <section class="founding-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">AMERICAN HISTORY & HERITAGE · FOUNDING ERA</p>
      <h1>The Birth of the United States</h1>
      <p class="lead">From thirteen British colonies to a new nation: the ideas, conflict, and difficult choices that shaped the American republic.</p>
      <p class="ru">От тринадцати британских колоний к новому государству: идеи, конфликт и сложные решения, сформировавшие американскую республику.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container founding-content">
    <a class="back-link" href="/american-english-method/learn/history-heritage/">← American History & Heritage</a>
    <div class="intro-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">WHY THIS STORY MATTERS</p>
      <p>The United States did not appear in a single moment. Its founding was a long process involving political disagreement, protest, war, diplomacy, and debate about how a new government should work. The story is also more complicated than a celebration of independence: the language of liberty and equality existed alongside slavery and major exclusions from political life.</p>
      <p class="ru">США не возникли в одно мгновение. Создание государства стало длительным процессом, включавшим политические разногласия, протесты, войну, дипломатию и споры о принципах управления. История независимости также сложнее торжественного рассказа о свободе: провозглашённые идеалы сосуществовали с рабством и ограничением политических прав.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 · BEFORE INDEPENDENCE</p>
      <h2>Why did the colonies break away from Britain?</h2>
      <p>By the middle of the eighteenth century, thirteen British colonies had developed along the Atlantic coast of North America. They had different economies, local governments, religious communities, and social structures. They were not one unified country.</p>
      <p>After the Seven Years' War ended in 1763, the British government faced significant debts and tried to raise revenue from its colonies. Measures such as the Stamp Act of 1765 increased tensions. Many colonists argued that Parliament should not tax them without their consent or representation. Others remained loyal to the British Crown, and many people did not support a break with Britain.</p>
      <p>Protests, boycotts, political arguments, and confrontations followed. In 1774, representatives from twelve colonies met at the First Continental Congress. Fighting began at Lexington and Concord in April 1775. The conflict had started, but independence had not yet been formally declared.</p>
      <p class="ru">К середине XVIII века вдоль атлантического побережья Северной Америки существовали тринадцать британских колоний. Они различались экономикой, местным управлением, религиозной и общественной жизнью. После окончания Семилетней войны в 1763 году британское правительство пыталось увеличить доходы и вводило новые сборы. Многие колонисты выступали против налогов без согласия и представительства. Однако не все поддерживали разрыв с Британией: среди жителей были и сторонники короны. Протесты и политические споры переросли в вооружённый конфликт в 1775 году.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 · JULY 4, 1776</p>
      <h2>A declaration — not the end of the war</h2>
      <p>On July 4, 1776, the Continental Congress adopted the Declaration of Independence. The document announced that the thirteen colonies considered themselves free and independent states. It also presented arguments about natural rights and the authority of government.</p>
      <p>The Declaration did not end the Revolutionary War. Fighting continued until 1783, when the Treaty of Paris formally recognized American independence. The Declaration became one of the most influential statements of American political ideals, but it did not establish the detailed structure of the federal government.</p>
      <div class="document-panel">
        <span class="number">PRIMARY SOURCE · 1776</span>
        <h3>The Declaration of Independence</h3>
        <p>One of its best-known ideas is that legitimate government depends on the consent of the people it governs.</p>
        <p class="ru">Одна из важнейших идей документа — законная власть должна опираться на согласие народа, которым она управляет.</p>
        <a href="https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/declaration-transcript" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the original transcription at the National Archives ↗</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 · BUILDING A GOVERNMENT</p>
      <h2>Independence raised a new question</h2>
      <p>Winning independence did not automatically answer a difficult question: how should the new states govern themselves together? The first national framework, the Articles of Confederation, created a relatively weak central government. Leaders and citizens debated how much authority a national government needed and how much power should remain with the states.</p>
      <p>In 1787, delegates met in Philadelphia and drafted a new Constitution. It established a federal system and divided national power among three branches: the legislative branch makes laws, the executive branch carries them out, and the judicial branch interprets the law. The Constitution was ratified in 1788 and the new federal government began operating in 1789.</p>
      <p>Some people worried that the new government might become too powerful. In response to demands for stronger protections, the first ten amendments — known as the Bill of Rights — were ratified in 1791.</p>
      <p class="ru">Победа в войне не решила вопрос о том, как новым штатам управлять страной сообща. Статьи Конфедерации создали сравнительно слабую центральную власть. В 1787 году делегаты разработали новую Конституцию, закрепившую федеральную систему и разделение власти на три ветви. Конституция была ратифицирована в 1788 году, а новое федеральное правительство начало работу в 1789-м. В 1791 году были ратифицированы первые десять поправок — Билль о правах.</p>
      <div class="document-links">
        <a href="https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the Constitution and founding documents at the National Archives ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/bill-of-rights-transcript" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the Bill of Rights transcription ↗</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">04 · IDEALS AND REALITY</p>
      <h2>Who was included in the promise of freedom?</h2>
      <p>The Declaration's language about equality and rights became central to American political culture. Yet in 1776, slavery remained legal in every colony that became a state, women did not have equal political rights, and Native nations faced pressure, conflict, dispossession, and the loss of land as the new republic expanded.</p>
      <p>People challenged these contradictions in different ways. Enslaved and free Black people petitioned, resisted, escaped, and served in the war. Women organized boycotts, managed businesses and farms, wrote political arguments, and contributed to the revolutionary effort, while remaining excluded from formal political power. Native nations made their own diplomatic and military decisions in a conflict that threatened their homelands.</p>
      <p>Understanding the founding era means studying both the ideals people proclaimed and the unequal realities people lived. The history of the United States includes generations of debate and struggle over who could enjoy the rights described in its founding documents.</p>
      <p class="ru">Язык равенства и прав стал важной частью американской политической культуры. Но в 1776 году рабство сохранялось во всех колониях, ставших штатами; женщины не обладали равными политическими правами; коренные народы сталкивались с давлением, войнами и потерей земель. История основания США — это одновременно история провозглашённых идеалов и борьба за то, чтобы эти идеалы распространялись на большее число людей.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">05 · A SHORT TIMELINE</p>
      <h2>From protest to a constitutional republic</h2>
      <div class="timeline">
        <article><span>1763</span><div><h3>Seven Years' War ends</h3><p>Britain faces war debts and new tensions with its colonies.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1775</span><div><h3>Armed conflict begins</h3><p>Fighting breaks out at Lexington and Concord.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1776</span><div><h3>Declaration of Independence</h3><p>Congress adopts the Declaration on July 4.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1783</span><div><h3>Treaty of Paris</h3><p>Britain formally recognizes American independence.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1787–89</span><div><h3>Constitution and new government</h3><p>The Constitution is drafted, ratified, and put into operation.</p></div></article>
        <article><span>1791</span><div><h3>Bill of Rights</h3><p>The first ten amendments are ratified.</p></div></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section language-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">AMERICAN ENGLISH · KEY VOCABULARY</p>
      <h2>Words to understand and use</h2>
      <div class="vocab-grid">
        <article><strong>colony</strong><span>колония</span><p>A territory controlled by another country.</p></article>
        <article><strong>to declare independence</strong><span>провозгласить независимость</span><p>To officially announce that a country is independent.</p></article>
        <article><strong>representation</strong><span>представительство</span><p>Having someone speak or act on your behalf in government.</p></article>
        <article><strong>to ratify</strong><span>ратифицировать</span><p>To formally approve an agreement or document.</p></article>
        <article><strong>to establish</strong><span>учредить, создать</span><p>To create something intended to last or operate.</p></article>
        <article><strong>individual rights</strong><span>права личности</span><p>Rights and freedoms that belong to each person.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div class="phrase"><strong>Natural expression:</strong> “have a say” — to have the opportunity to express an opinion or influence a decision.<br /><span>Пример: “People wanted to have a say in how they were governed.” — Люди хотели иметь возможность влиять на то, как ими управляют.</span></div>
    </section>

    <section class="lesson-section practice-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING</p>
      <h2>Think, describe, and speak</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Why did tensions grow between Britain and the thirteen colonies?</li>
        <li>What did the Declaration of Independence announce — and what did it not create?</li>
        <li>Why did the new country need a Constitution?</li>
        <li>Why was the Bill of Rights added?</li>
        <li>Which groups did not enjoy equal rights in the founding era?</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="speaking-prompt">Speaking challenge: Explain the difference between declaring independence and establishing a government in four or five sentences. Try to use <em>independence</em>, <em>representation</em>, <em>ratify</em>, and <em>individual rights</em>.</p>
      <p class="ru">Задание: объясните в четырёх-пяти предложениях разницу между провозглашением независимости и созданием системы государственного управления. Постарайтесь использовать новые английские слова.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="sources-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">SOURCES & FURTHER READING</p>
      <h2>Explore the original records</h2>
      <ul>
